<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
A method marked with <code>[System.Web.Services.WebMethod]</code> is an ASMX web service operation
or, when it is a static method of a page, an ASP.NET page method. Any client that can reach the
service can call it. A web method that changes data, such as one that transfers, withdraws,
deposits, updates, removes, edits or deletes something, or that performs an administrative action,
should check that the caller is allowed to do so.
</p>
<p>
This query reports such a web method when it has no authorization check in its code, no
authorization attribute that applies to it, and no <code>&lt;location&gt;</code> rule in a
<code>Web.config</code> file that denies access to it. The standard query
<code>cs/web/missing-function-level-access-control</code> does not consider web methods.
</p>
<p>
The MVC and Web API <code>Authorize</code> attributes (<code>System.Web.Mvc.AuthorizeAttribute</code>
and <code>System.Web.Http.AuthorizeAttribute</code>) are filters of their own pipelines. They do
not run for a web method, so they do not protect it.
</p>

</overview>
<recommendation>

<p>
Check that the caller is authenticated and authorized before changing data, for example with
<code>Context.User.Identity.IsAuthenticated</code> or <code>Context.User.IsInRole(...)</code>. Alternatively,
deny access to unauthorized users with an <code>authorization</code> element in a
<code>&lt;location&gt;</code> element of <code>Web.config</code> that covers the service.
</p>

</recommendation>
<example>

<p>
In the following example, any caller can transfer funds between arbitrary accounts:
</p>

<sample language="csharp"><![CDATA[
[WebMethod]
public string TransferBalance(long from, long to, double amount)
{
    Withdraw(from, amount);
    Deposit(to, amount);
    return "OK";
}
]]></sample>

<p>
The method is fixed by checking that the caller is authenticated (and, ideally, owns the debited
account):
</p>

<sample language="csharp"><![CDATA[
[WebMethod]
public string TransferBalance(long from, long to, double amount)
{
    if (!Context.User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
        return "Unauthorized";
    Withdraw(from, amount);
    Deposit(to, amount);
    return "OK";
}
]]></sample>

<p>
The following <code>Web.config</code> file denies anonymous users access to the service:
</p>

<sample language="xml"><![CDATA[
<configuration>
  <location path="AccountService.asmx">
    <system.web>
      <authorization>
        <deny users="?" />
      </authorization>
    </system.web>
  </location>
</configuration>
]]></sample>

</example>
<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/">Broken Access Control</a>.</li>
